 {"id":"CF-178","title":"P3 - the auto-disabled lane's durable pin still says enabled=true: the DB row, fleet.toml and the repo's lane loader disagree about a parked code-factory-qa","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"e1d0b4acacf35eb2fca789d3a436f2a186c4627b2e8b1f52bea80655f0ba962c"},"reasoning":"The paragraph's provenance section (docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md:142-147) tells the operator that a DB value has a durable ~/.hermes/fleet.toml pin behind it, and the same section is the one an operator uses to decide whether a live pacing value can be trusted. For code-factory-qa the two layers disagree and no record explains it: the live row is enabled=0 / disabled_by=auto-disable (disabled_at 2026-11-04T06:03:49Z, \"failure rate 91.3% (84/92 ticks, window 100, threshold 0.90)\"), while fleet.toml:2118 still reads enabled = true for the lane whose stanza is otherwise byte-current (cooldown_s = 1800, namespace code-factory).\n\nThe repo's only lane writer, scripts/load_lane_config.py, never reads disabled_at (0 occurrences), so nothing in the repo can notice that a lane it is about to configure has been parked by the scheduler, and the park exists only in the live DB. Whether a config reload actually re-enables the lane is NOT measured here and the row does not claim it; the measurable facts are the disagreement, the absence of a durable record of the park, and the writer that cannot see it. The fix is a decision recorded where the doc already promises one — which layer owns enabled after an auto-disable — plus a refusal in the writer so the park cannot be erased by a lane-config run.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `grep -c 'disabled_at' scripts/load_lane_config.py` prints `0`; `sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name,enabled,disabled_by from projects where name='code-factory-qa'\"` prints `code-factory-qa|0|auto-disable`; `grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled` prints `enabled = true` — the live row and its durable pin disagree, and nothing in the repo records the park.\n1. The repo states which layer owns `enabled` after an auto-disable and where the park is recorded: a documented decision in docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md naming the auto-disable fields (`disabled_at`, `disabled_by`, `disabled_reason`) and saying whether the durable `fleet.toml` pin is authoritative or is expected to go stale, so a reader can tell without guessing.\n2. After the change the two layers agree, and the row note quotes the re-run of acceptance criterion 0 showing one value for both. If the durable pin is authoritative, the lane is re-enabled and the note names the mechanism and the actor; otherwise the pin is flipped in the same change and the note names where that flip is recorded.\n3. `scripts/load_lane_config.py` refuses to write a lane whose row is parked (`disabled_by` non-empty) unless `--re-arm` is passed, so the only repo-side lane writer cannot silently restore a lane the scheduler parked; the refusal names the lane and writes nothing.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0, and the script change carries a selftest arm that goes red without the parked-lane refusal (fixture DB copy, parked row, no `--re-arm`).","probe":"grep -c 'disabled_at' scripts/load_lane_config.py; sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name, enabled, disabled_by from projects where name='code-factory-qa';\"; grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled"}
 {"id":"CF-179","title":"P4 — node-create.sh's success line prints a literal /home/<uid> placeholder, so the manifest path it hands the operator resolves nowhere","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":1,"created_at":"2026-11-04T08:05:35Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T08:05:35Z","detail":{"lane":"code-factory-dogfood","tick":"code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-04-07-25-03","evidence":"~/.hermes/stand-in/dogfood/code-factory/dogfood-2026-11-04-evidence.md#9"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 on a throwaway clone checked out at published tip c1896cc (the walk ran in the copy because five family lanes held the shared checkout). `scripts/node-create.sh:130` ends the successful create path with `say \"manifest: /home/<uid>/.node/node.json\"`. `<uid>` is a sanitisation artifact, not a value the script ever substitutes: the same line is byte-identical in the working tree and in `git show c1896cc:scripts/node-create.sh`, and no assignment or `$HOME` expansion exists on that line. The message is printed on the CONTROL host but names a path that only exists on the node (`specs/NODE.md:5` \"Manifest (`$HOME/.node/node.json`)\", `docs/OPERATIONS.md:107`, and `scripts/project-node.sh:297` writes `\"$STATE/node.json\"`), so an operator who follows it runs `cat` against a literal `<uid>` directory on the wrong machine. It is the last thing a successful create prints, which is exactly the moment a user needs the artifact. Duplicate check at c1896cc: `jq -r select(.title+\" \"+((.detail//\"\")|tostring)|test(\"manifest|node\\\\.json|<uid>\"))` over `.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` returns five rows (CF-066, CF-078, CF-087, CF-162 and one more) and none names this message; `grep -c '<uid>' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 0 for the success-line defect. Same class as CF-160 (evidence strings that are not re-runnable) on the operator-facing surface.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the pre-fix tree, from the repo root: `grep -n 'manifest:' scripts/node-create.sh` prints the success line containing the literal `/home/<uid>/.node/node.json`. Prove the named path is unusable, not merely ugly: `test -e '/home/<uid>/.node/node.json'; echo \"exists_rc=$?\"` prints `exists_rc=1`, and `python3 -c 'import os; print(os.path.realpath(\"/home/<uid>/.node/node.json\"))'` shows the literal `<uid>` component is never expanded.\n1. After the fix the success line names a real, fully-resolved path and says which machine holds it: `grep -c '<uid>' scripts/node-create.sh` prints `0`, and the emitted line interpolates the node's own home (for example `manifest: $HOME/.node/node.json on <AGENT>`) plus the command that reads it back, `bunker exec --server <server> <agent> -- cat ~/.node/node.json`.\n2. A gate arm proves the message cannot regress: `tests/selftest.sh` gains a check that drives the success-summary path with a stub `bunker` and a stub `verify-node.sh`, and fails when the emitted manifest path contains `<` or fails to name the node as its location. The arm must FAIL against the current line and PASS after the fix; a green arm that was never red is not evidence.\n3. `bash tests/selftest.sh` exits 0 at the fixed tree, and `scripts/node-create.sh --staged-files` still exits 0 (the fix must not disturb the staging manifest).\n4. The row closes only against the commit that satisfies 1-3, with the transcript of 0 and 1 in the evidence file.","probe":"grep -n 'manifest: /home/<uid>' scripts/node-create.sh && test -e '/home/<uid>/.node/node.json'; echo \"named_path_exists=$?\""}
 {"id":"CF-180","title":"P4 \u2014 a board-only commit silently absorbs a sibling lane's uncommitted event line: 50b4ba3 carries event 285 and names only 284","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T08:20:54Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T08:20:54Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"c535c477e223ce23b532705dc3b1b16cf97cc21e1cbeb52f78052dfa2a1307cd"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04T03:17-05:00 in the shared checkout /home/kara/code-factory. boardctl appended this lane's audit event (id 285, task CF-099) at 08:17:35Z while the sibling code-factory-dogfood lane had appended event 284 at 08:17:19Z to the same file; neither line was committed at that moment. The dogfood lane then committed the file as 50b4ba3 (\"board(dogfood): append third-pass verification event for CF-179 (event 284)\") and `git show --stat 50b4ba3` prints `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | 2 ++`, so the commit carries BOTH 284 and 285 while its message and its ch:trace name only its own lane: `git log -1 --format=%B 50b4ba3 | grep -c '285\\|CF-099'` prints 0. A reader who wants the provenance of event 285 finds it inside a commit that never mentions it, and the same shape can absorb any number of lanes' transitions without a message ever being wrong enough to look wrong.\n\nThis is structural, not a one-off: events.jsonl is a single append-only file shared by every lane in the family, boardctl serializes the WRITE (board lock, adjudicated by scripts/board_lock.py) but nothing binds a commit's message to the lines that commit picks up, and docs/BOARD.md's write rules 1-5 say nothing about the case (`grep -c 'absorbed\\|names every added' docs/BOARD.md` = 0). The commit that absorbs a sibling's line is a normal, well-formed board-only commit; only the message understates its tree.\n\nDistinct from CF-119 (a present .lock is not proof of a held lock) because here no lock is involved and no lane should stand down: the hazard is at commit time, after both writes succeeded. Distinct from CF-160 (evidence strings that do not re-run) because the quoted evidence here does re-run and reproduces the mis-attribution exactly. Cost observed on this tick: the PM lane's audit event landed inside another lane's commit, so the lane could not name a commit of its own, and 50b4ba3's message and stat line disagree about what it contains.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the published tree: `git show --stat 50b4ba3` prints `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | 2 ++` (1 file, 2 insertions); `git show 50b4ba3:.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | tail -2 | jq -r '.id'` prints 284 and 285; `git log -1 --format=%B 50b4ba3 | grep -c '285\\|CF-099'` prints 0 \u2014 the commit carries a second lane's audit event and never names it.\n1. docs/BOARD.md carries an explicit write rule: whoever commits events.jsonl must read back every line the commit adds and name each absorbed event id together with its lane/task in the commit message, or leave the shared file to the lane that wrote the line; a commit that carries more than one lane's transition must not be described as single-lane.\n2. The rule ships with a re-runnable recipe (documented command text, no new daemon) that lists the event ids a commit adds and flags any id its message does not name; running that exact recipe against 50b4ba3 prints both 284 and 285 and marks 285 unnamed.\n3. tests/selftest.sh gains an arm that runs the documented recipe against a fixture repository whose commit adds two event ids while naming one, and fails when the recipe stops flagging the unnamed id \u2014 the arm is RED before the doc rule and recipe exist and GREEN after, and the row's evidence quotes both runs.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0 and `scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fix commit.","probe":"printf 'hist_added='; git show 50b4ba3:.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | tail -2 | jq -r '.id' | paste -sd,; printf 'hist_named='; git log -1 --format=%B 50b4ba3 | grep -oE 'event 28[0-9]|CF-[0-9]+' | sort -u | paste -sd,; printf 'rule_mentioned='; grep -c 'absorbed' docs/BOARD.md; true"}
+{"id":"CF-181","title":"P3 — the PRD's requirement-id column and the board's task-id column share the CF-nnn namespace, so the milestone blocker list names CF-110/CF-112 that have never existed as rows and 'the milestone cannot be claimed while any row above is open' is undecidable for those two entries","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T08:32:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T08:32:00Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"88f6a7c404f4114e671713fc49da94219694567dd06f9a03dc0f54c7fea60f17"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-pm tick (07-36-30, third pass) from a fresh clone of origin/main at 93ef524. `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` opens a requirements table whose ID column runs CF-101, CF-102, ... (`grep -c '^| CF-1' docs/PRD-code-factory.md` = 12 at 93ef524). Those are REQUIREMENT ids: the board carries no row for them (`jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l` = 0 and the same for CF-112). The milestone section then reuses the identical spelling for board rows: 'The open defects this milestone's legs depend on are CF-080 ..., the never-exercised legs CF-001 ..., CF-004 ..., CF-110 (no destroy path ships) and CF-112 (no dispatch-targets.jsonl consumer ships)' and closes with 'The milestone cannot be claimed while any row above is open.' A reader who runs the check the same section prescribes — `jq -r 'select(.status!=\"complete\") | [.id,.priority] | @tsv' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` — cannot find CF-110 or CF-112 at all: `grep -c 'CF-110' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 2 matches only inside other rows' prose, `jq -r 'select(.task_id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | wc -l` = 0, and `git log --all --oneline -S'\"id\":\"CF-110\"' -- .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` is empty, so no ref has ever carried such a row. The two spellings are indistinguishable by shape, so the milestone's own membership rule ('every open row whose failure stops one of the four legs ... must appear here') is not machine-decidable for exactly those two entries, and a future tick cannot tell a dangling blocker from a requirement. Distinct from every open row: `grep -c 'CF-181' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 0 before this row was filed, and CF-175 covers a different defect (the deployed lane prompt names a PRD path no ref carries), not the id namespace.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce at any tip from the repo root: the row's probe prints `req_ids_in_prd:` > 0, `cf110_row: 0`, `cf112_row: 0`, `cf110_events: 0`, `cited_in_milestone:` > 0 and exits 0. `git log --all --oneline -S'\"id\":\"CF-110\"' -- .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` is empty (no ref ever carried such a row).\n1. The Requirements preamble in `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` states that its ID column is a requirement namespace and NOT the board's task-id namespace, so a CF-nnn in that column need not exist as a row; `grep -c 'requirement namespace' docs/PRD-code-factory.md` >= 1.\n2. Every CF-nnn cited in the milestone blocker list resolves deterministically: for each id the check prints either `row=<status>` (from `jq` over the board) or `requirement-only` (from the requirements table), and it exits 0 only when every cited board row is non-complete and every requirement-only entry carries a testless `Verified by` cell. Ship the check as a committed script (e.g. `docs/audits/prd-blocker-resolution.sh`) so a stranger can re-run it; `bash <script>` exits 0 at the fix commit and exits nonzero when one cited id is deleted from both the board and the table (prove the negative by deleting one id in a scratch copy, not in the tree).\n3. A gate arm fails when the PRD cites a CF-nnn that resolves to neither namespace: add the case to `tests/selftest.sh` (or to the docs audit gate `docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh` if it is the shipped home for PRD checks), RED against the pre-fix PRD and GREEN after, with the row id in the arm's name.\n4. At the fix commit `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0 and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` (pre-existing free-form-vocabulary warnings excepted).","probe":"printf 'req_ids_in_prd: '; grep -c '^| CF-1' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; printf 'cf110_row: '; jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cf112_row: '; jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-112\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cf110_events: '; jq -r 'select(.task_id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cited_in_milestone: '; grep -c 'CF-110\\|CF-112' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; printf 'namespace_legend: '; grep -c 'requirement namespace' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; true"}
